%S A399758 22,33311012,33311014,33311015,33311016,33311017,33311018,33311019,
%T A399758 33311210,33311214,33311215,33311216,33311217,33311218,33311219,
%U A399758 33311410,33311412,33311415,33311416,33311417,33311418,33311419,33311510,33311512,33311514,33311516,33311517
%N A399758 Fixed points with respect to the 'say what you see' transformation described in A063850.
%C A399758 Also referred to as self-inventoried numbers (see Rivera link).
%C A399758 There are clusters of terms with 19 digits or more, such as 1111013141516171819, 1111013141516171912, etc. and 221111013141516171819, 221111013141516171918, etc.
%C A399758 The map in A063850 inventories digits in the order they are encountered. Hence, 10213223 in A047841 is not a term here since it would be summarized by the map in A063850 as 21103223. - _Michael S. Branicky_, Nov 10 2026
%H A399758 Paolo P. Lava, <a href="/A399758/b399758.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..267</a>
%H A399758 Carlos Rivera, <a href="https://www.primepuzzles.net/puzzles/puzz_207.htm">Puzzle 207. The inventory sequences and the self-inventoried numbers</a>, The Prime Puzzles and Problems Connection.
%e A399758 22 is a term since there are two 2s, summarized as 22 itself;
%e A399758 33311012 is a term since there are three 3s, three 1s, one 0, and one 2 or 33311012.
%Y A399758 Cf. A047841, A063850, A109776.
